From: pdx203 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Subject: bridge: count surfaces in use while command buffers use them, not while textures live

The render server counted a surface in use (IOSurfaceIncrementUseCount) for as long as a texture over it lived, and Core Animation keeps
a client surface's texture about four frames after its last use (64 ms on average in landscape TestUFO). Every buffer WebKit had handed
over still looked in use, so its pool could reuse none: it made a new 6 MB surface for nearly every frame, which its GPU process and the
render server each imported, mapped and let go again (about 95 imports a second, 2 s of kernel time in 10 s). Apple's GPU driver marks a
surface in use while submitted work references it; a command buffer of the render server now counts each distinct surface its textures
lie over from its commit to its completion (2-8 ms on average). Steady TestUFO makes no surface import at all (920-1070 map/unmap calls
in 10 s before), total CPU 210% -> ~150%.

The import diagnostics that found it: every 2 s at most, at an import of 1 MiB or more, a GPUNOTE line through Turnip's tu_darwin_note
(Adreno650 0026) with such imports, held imports let go and how long surfaces stayed counted in use.

